steps.message:
This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   The user reported a bug in this repository (Recharts charting library, /app, version 3.9.2) titled "Full-circle polar chart is inconsistent with its own axis," submitted in a single message (from before this conversation window; verbatim, preserved from the prior summary since it defines the acceptance criteria):

   "# Full-circle polar chart is inconsistent with its own axis

   I have a polar chart whose angular axis spans the whole circle, one categorical series per spoke,
   with a tooltip enabled.

   Hovering it gives me this, and the result depends on which part of the circle I point at:

   ![image](/app/.harbor_assets/bug.png)

   And this is what it draws for the data I passed in, with no pointer involved at all:

   ![image](/app/.harbor_assets/mismatch.png)

   ## What is the expected behavior?

   The chart should be self-consistent everywhere on the circle: for any spoke, the series it is
   labelled with, the series drawn on it, and the series a hover near it resolves to must all be the
   same one — and a hover must resolve to the spoke nearest the pointer, reporting that series' own
   point on the chart.

   This has to hold for any number of series, and whatever start and end angle the circle is laid out
   with."

   No further explicit user messages were sent in this conversation window; all work in this window was my own autonomous continuation of the investigation and fix per the previous session's "Optional Next Step" directive, with no new user requests to address. (The only other "user" turn in this window was a system-level meta-instruction directing me to produce a structured summary — not a substantive request about the bug.)

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Recharts internal architecture: Redux-based state selectors (reselect `createSelector`), organized under `/app/src/state/selectors/*.ts`.
   - Polar/Radar chart rendering pipeline: `PolarChart.tsx` → `RadarChart.tsx` (sets default `startAngle=90`, `endAngle=-270`) → `Radar.tsx` (draws polygon via `computeRadarPoints`) → `PolarAngleAxis.tsx` (renders axis line + tick labels).
   - d3 `scaleBand` (via `victory-vendor/d3-scale`) used for categorical angle axis; its `.bandwidth()` is always non-negative regardless of range direction (empirically confirmed: bandwidth=90 for 4 equal categories regardless of whether range is `[90,-270]`, `[0,360]`, `[-270,90]`, or `[360,0]`).
   - `RechartsScale` wrapper (`/app/src/util/scale/RechartsScale.ts`) around d3 scales.
   - The "four horsemen of tick generation" — a comment embedded in the codebase (axisSelectors.ts, tooltipSelectors.ts, ChartUtils.ts) flagging four near-duplicated tick-generation implementations: `combineTicksOfTooltipAxis` (tooltipSelectors.ts), `combineAxisTicks` (axisSelectors.ts, rendered axis labels), `getTicksOfAxis` (ChartUtils.ts), and `combineGraphicalItemTicks` (axisSelectors.ts).
   - `mathSign()` utility (`/app/src/util/DataUtils.ts`): returns -1, 0, or 1 based on sign of input.
   - Angle-axis-specific "offset" logic pattern (found in `combineAxisTicks`/`combineTicksOfTooltipAxis`/`getTicksOfAxis`): `offset = axisType === 'angleAxis' && axisRange != null ? mathSign(axisRange[0] - axisRange[1]) * 2 * offset : offset`.
   - `getBandSizeOfAxis` (`/app/src/util/ChartUtils.ts`): returns `scale.bandwidth()` directly when available — always positive, ignoring range direction/sign. This is used by many consumers (bar, line, area, radialBar, radar) and was NOT modified (to avoid regressing those other consumers); instead the sign-correction was applied locally at the one call site that needed it (`computeRadarPoints`).
   - `computeRadarPoints` (`/app/src/polar/Radar.tsx`): computes each data point's angle; root cause of the bug was here — it added an always-positive `angleBandSize` (derived from `getBandSizeOfAxis`) to `angleAxis.scale.map(name)`, ignoring axis range direction, while the axis tick labels/tooltip ticks correctly flip this offset's sign based on `mathSign(axisRange[0] - axisRange[1])`.
   - `selectRadarPoints` / `selectAngleAxisWithScaleAndViewport` (`/app/src/state/selectors/radarSelectors.ts`): selector chain feeding `computeRadarPoints`.
   - `selectAngleAxisRangeWithReversed` (`/app/src/state/selectors/polarAxisSelectors.ts`): selector giving the axis range (accounting for `reversed` prop), reused as the new dependency to compute the sign.
   - Vitest test infrastructure: `createSelectorTestCase` (test/helper/createSelectorTestCase.tsx) for spying on selector values inside rendered components; `mockGetBoundingClientRect` and `showTooltipOnCoordinate`/`getTooltip` (test/component/Tooltip/tooltipTestHelpers.tsx) for simulating mouse hover and reading tooltip content in jsdom.
   - Regression-test verification methodology: using `git stash push -- <files>` / `git stash pop` to temporarily revert only the source fix (not the new tests) and confirm the new tests fail without the fix and pass with it — proving the tests are a genuine regression guard, not tautological.

3. Files and Code Sections:
   - `/app/src/polar/Radar.tsx` (MODIFIED — core fix)
     - Added import: `import { interpolate, isNullish, mathSign, noop } from '../util/DataUtils';` (added `mathSign` to existing import).
     - Added import: `import { AxisRange } from '../state/selectors/axisSelectors';` (new import, added after the `usePolarChartLayout` import; verified no circular dependency exists since `axisSelectors.ts` does not import from `polar/Radar.tsx`).
     - Modified `AngleAxisForRadar` type to add a `range` field:
       ```ts
       export type RadiusAxisForRadar = { scale: RechartsScale };
       export type AngleAxisForRadar = {
         scale: RechartsScale;
         type: 'number' | 'category';
         dataKey: DataKey<any> | undefined;
         cx: number;
         cy: number;
         range: AxisRange | undefined;
       };
       ```
     - Modified `computeRadarPoints` function:
       ```ts
       export function computeRadarPoints({
         radiusAxis,
         angleAxis,
         displayedData,
         dataKey,
         bandSize,
       }: {
         radiusAxis: RadiusAxisForRadar;
         angleAxis: AngleAxisForRadar;
         displayedData: any[];
         dataKey: RadarProps['dataKey'];
         bandSize: number;
       }): RadarComposedData {
         const { cx, cy, range } = angleAxis;
         let isRange = false;
         const points: RadarPoint[] = [];
         /*
          * The angle axis scale (a d3 scaleBand) always maps to the start of each category's band,
          * regardless of which direction the axis range runs in - scale.bandwidth() is always positive.
          * The rendered tick labels (and therefore the tooltip, which reads ticks from the very same
          * source) are centered within their band by adding a signed offset - the sign flips depending on
          * whether the axis range increases or decreases (see combineAxisTicks / combineTicksOfTooltipAxis).
          * We need to replicate that same sign here, otherwise the drawn polygon vertices land on a
          * different spoke than the axis label and tooltip agree on, whenever the axis range increases
          * (e.g. startAngle=0, endAngle=360) instead of the default decreasing direction.
          */
         const bandSizeSign = range != null && range.length >= 2 ? mathSign(range[0] - range[1]) : 1;
         const angleBandSize = angleAxis.type !== 'number' ? bandSizeSign * (bandSize ?? 0) : 0;

         displayedData.forEach((entry, i) => {
           const name = getValueByDataKey(entry, angleAxis.dataKey, i);
           const value = getValueByDataKey(entry, dataKey);
           const angle: number = (angleAxis.scale.map(name) ?? 0) + angleBandSize;
           // ... (rest unchanged)
         });
         // ... (rest unchanged)
       }
       ```
   - `/app/src/state/selectors/radarSelectors.ts` (MODIFIED — wires the range through to `computeRadarPoints`)
     - Added import: `selectAngleAxisRangeWithReversed` to the existing import from `./polarAxisSelectors`:
       ```ts
       import {
         selectAngleAxis,
         selectAngleAxisRangeWithReversed,
         selectPolarViewBox,
         selectRadiusAxis,
       } from './polarAxisSelectors';
       ```
     - Added `AxisRange` to the existing import from `./axisSelectors`: `import { AxisRange, BaseAxisWithScale } from './axisSelectors';`
     - Added new selector and updated `selectAngleAxisWithScaleAndViewport`:
       ```ts
       const selectAngleAxisRangeForRadar = (
         state: RechartsRootState,
         _radiusAxisId: AxisId,
         angleAxisId: AxisId,
       ): AxisRange | undefined => selectAngleAxisRangeWithReversed(state, angleAxisId);

       export const selectAngleAxisWithScaleAndViewport: (
         state: RechartsRootState,
         _radiusAxisId: AxisId,
         angleAxisId: AxisId,
       ) => AngleAxisForRadar | undefined = createSelector(
         [selectAngleAxisForRadar, selectPolarAxisScaleForRadar, selectPolarViewBox, selectAngleAxisRangeForRadar],
         (
           axisOptions: AngleAxisSettings,
           scale: RechartsScale | undefined,
           polarViewBox: PolarViewBoxRequired | undefined,
           range: AxisRange | undefined,
         ): AngleAxisForRadar | undefined => {
           if (polarViewBox == null || scale == null) {
             return undefined;
           }
           return {
             scale,
             type: axisOptions.type,
             dataKey: axisOptions.dataKey,
             cx: polarViewBox.cx,
             cy: polarViewBox.cy,
             range,
